 after the step — `snapshot` returns `Outcome::Screen` with the screen's text, or, for a screen over the snapshot budget or a `part` asked, `Outcome::Part` with one part's text and the counts `part` and `parts`, and an instance that did not go quiet is a result — `settled` false with its `busy` class — not rolled back. Since 2026-10-12-headless-screenshot `run` also takes `screenshot`, the seventh instance verb, its `id` optional, and returns `Outcome::Shot { width, height, png }` — a PNG of the harness's raster, the whole screen or the element's bounds cropped to the part that can be seen and rounded outward — with no diff, a shot changing nothing (`has_changes` false, focus and the next snapshot unchanged); its `id` is looked up as a `click`'s is, refused `stale` or `not-found`, a crop of no pixel is refused `off-screen`, and an app with no font it can paint text with is refused `fonts-unavailable`, the eighth cause, with nothing painted (packages/escher-driver/src/execute.rs:79-87; packages/escher-driver/src/execute.rs:474-495). `with_time(step)` hands the session its caller's time step, the step moving the app's own time; every session accepts `advance`, which moves the session's clock by exactly `ms` and then runs the step where one was handed, `advanced_ms` reading `ms` (ratified by the founder, 2026-10-18). Since 2026-10-10-driver-cli: a session-level verb (`start`, `status`, `stop`) handed to `run` is refused `unknown-verb`; `type` replaces — after the focusing click (a select, single or `multiple`, focused with none and no DOM focus event fired, so a text opening on a space types nothing on a single select, packages/escher-driver/src/execute.rs:557-563), where the snapshot reads a `value` for the target (an empty text input reads the empty string), the driver presses the engine's select-all and then types, and with an empty text deletes the selection, so an empty text clears the control; and the record of ids holds no id longer than `MAX_ID_BYTES`. `act` and `with_time` are in process; `run` is also the one thing a host does with a `call` request, so that call and its answer cross the socket (session-wire), and since 2026-10-10-mcp-surface what the MCP server does with an instance verb's tool call, so that call and its result cross the `mcp` role's stdio (architecture §Standard Contracts → Driver session); since 2026-10-19-session-recording `Session::recording_into(dir)` (unix only, `Unsupported` elsewhere) creates `dir` `0700` or refuses `RecordDirNotPrivate`, then `check-{n}.json` `0600` with `create_new`, never replacing a file, and `run` records each answered call of the eight instance verbs, a `lint` among them — `tool` · `arguments` in schema order · `masked` (a `type` on a masked or unresolved target, its text the stand-in `MASKED_STAND_IN`) · `accepted` · `digest` (`answer_digest`, FNV-1a 64 over the whole written line, a shot's PNG included) · `answer` (since 2026-10-19-recorded-check-replay: the kept line as its own JSON object, masked as on every carrier, a shot's without `png`, `{"width":…,"height":…}`) — rewriting the check whole through a `0600` temporary and a rename; past 400 records or 1,048,576 bytes, or on a failed write, the recording ends, its check removed, and `recording_failed()` reads true once, the call not rolled back; `Check::from_bytes` reads a kept check strictly, `answer` required as a JSON object, anything else refused `malformed`; since 2026-10-19-recorded-check-replay `Check::replay(&mut Session) -> Replay` runs nothing on a session whose label is not the check's app, otherwise runs each step's call through `Session::run`, validated again, and stops at the first step whose acceptance differs or whose live kept line, parsed as a JSON value, is not equal to the kept `answer` — `Replay` (`holds` · `another_app` · `ran` · `change`) and `Change` (`step` · `accepted_then` · `accepted_now` · `keys`, the verb's result fields that differ in table order, then `refused`) holding no reading and no `serde_json` type; `run`'s signature and its `command` span are unchanged — ratified by the founder (2026-10-19, F1 A · F2 B, F3 B) (packages/escher-driver/src/recording.rs:134-136; packages/escher-driver/src/replay.rs:40-70; packages/escher-driver/src/recording.rs:145-190; packages/escher-driver/src/session.rs:66-244; packages/escher-driver/src/execute.rs:114-309).
